course: world
lesson: 73
title: Birds: feathers, nests and flight
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Feathers are made of keratin, the same stuff as our hair and nails. Long, stiff flight feathers on the wings and tail catch the air, and soft, fluffy down feathers close to the skin trap warm air like a duvet.
explain: A flying bird must be light and strong: many of its bones are hollow, with thin struts inside like a bridge, and it has no heavy teeth, and its huge flight muscles are fixed to a tall keel on its breastbone.
explain: A wing is curved, so as the bird moves forward the air flowing past pushes it upward; this push is called lift. Flapping pushes air down and back, so the bird is pushed up and forward.
explain: A bird's beak shows what it eats: a hooked beak tears meat, a short thick beak cracks seeds, a long thin beak reaches nectar in flowers and a flat beak sifts food from water.
explain: For example, a bird preens every day, combing its feathers with its beak and spreading oil from a gland near its tail, so a duck stays dry even after a dive.
fact: Feathers are made of keratin, the same material as hair and nails.
fact: Down feathers trap air and keep a bird warm.
fact: Many of a bird's bones are hollow, which makes it light for flying.
fact: A bird's strong flight muscles are fixed to a keel on its breastbone.
fact: The curved shape of a wing gives lift as air flows past it.
fact: Birds have no teeth; a muscly part of the stomach called the gizzard grinds their food, often with swallowed grit.
fact: Long narrow wings are good for gliding, like an albatross's, and broad wings are good for soaring on rising warm air, like an eagle's.
fact: A hummingbird can hover by beating its wings dozens of times a second.
fact: Birds preen to keep their feathers clean, tidy and waterproof.
fact: Birds keep their eggs warm with their own bodies until the chicks hatch.
fact: Birds build many kinds of nests: cups of twigs, woven baskets, mud nests and holes in trees.
fact: A cuckoo lays its eggs in other birds' nests and lets them raise its chicks.
fact: Birds are living descendants of dinosaurs.
exercise: What are feathers made of? | Keratin, like hair and nails | Bone, like a skeleton | Wood fibres, like paper | Plastic made by the bird's skin
exercise: What do down feathers do? | Trap air to keep the bird warm | Steer the bird when it turns | Help the bird swim under water | Make the bird look bigger to enemies
exercise: Why are many of a bird's bones hollow? | To make the bird light enough to fly | To store food for the winter | To make the bird's song louder | To hold water inside them for long flights
exercise: Where are a bird's strong flight muscles fixed? | To the keel on its breastbone | To the hollow bones of its legs | To the very tips of its wings | To its long stiff tail feathers
exercise: What gives a bird's wing lift? | Its curved shape as air flows past | The warmth of the bird's body below it | The weight of its feathers | The flapping of its tail
exercise: How do birds grind their food without teeth? | In a muscly gizzard, often with swallowed grit | By chewing it with their tongue | By hitting it on stones for hours | They cannot eat anything hard at all, only soft food
exercise: What does a hooked beak, like an eagle's, show? | The bird tears meat | The bird cracks seeds | The bird sips nectar | The bird sifts mud
exercise: What kind of beak is best for cracking seeds? | A short, thick beak | A long, thin beak | A flat, wide beak | A long, hooked beak
exercise: Why does a bird preen? | To keep its feathers clean, tidy and waterproof | To sharpen its beak for eating | To make its feathers grow faster and longer each day | To cool down on hot days
exercise: How can a hummingbird hover in front of a flower? | By beating its wings dozens of times a second | By holding its wings still | By floating on the scent of the flower | By resting its feet on the petals
exercise: Which wings are best for gliding over the sea for hours? | Long and narrow, like an albatross's | Short and round, like a sparrow's wings | Tiny and stiff, like a penguin's flippers | Broad and soft, like a quiet owl's wings
exercise: How do birds keep their eggs warm? | They sit on them and warm them with their bodies | They bury them deep in hot sand and leave them there | The sunlight alone warms them | The eggs make their own heat
exercise: What does a cuckoo do with its eggs? | Lays them in other birds' nests | Carries them around on its back as it flies | Buries them in warm sand | Hides them in caves
exercise: From which animals did birds come? | Dinosaurs | Bats | Insects | Fish
test: A duck dives under the water and comes up with dry feathers underneath. Why? | Its feathers are oiled and waterproof from preening | Ducks have no feathers at all under their wings and belly | The water is too cold to wet feathers | Ducks dive too briefly to get wet
test: A bird has a long, thin, curved beak. What does it most likely eat? | Nectar from deep flowers | Big hard seeds and nuts from trees | Meat from other small animals | Little fish sifted from the mud
test: A coat filled with feathers is very warm. Which feathers are used and why? | Down, because it traps a lot of air | Flight feathers, because they are stiff | Tail feathers, because they are long | Any feathers, because all feathers are hot
test: A bird with very broad wings circles high without flapping on a sunny day. How does it stay up? | It rides on warm air rising from the ground | It is lighter than the air around it | Its hollow bones are filled with a light gas like a balloon | It is pulled up by the Sun
test: If a bird's bones were solid and heavy like ours, what would happen? | Flying would be much harder | It would fly faster and higher | It would need no wings at all | It would stay warmer in winter
test: A small bird is busy feeding a chick much bigger than itself in its nest. What might explain this? | A cuckoo laid its egg in that nest | The chick is the small bird's own baby | The small bird found the chick on the ground | The chick grew big from extra warmth
test: Why do paper aeroplane wings and real plane wings work a bit like a bird's wing? | Air flowing past the wings pushes them up | They are made of feathers | They flap up and down just like a bird's wings | They are lighter than air
test: A chicken swallows small stones. Why? | The stones help its gizzard grind food | The stones make it heavier in wind | The stones clean its beak | The stones keep its feathers clean
test: Which is true about birds and dinosaurs? | Birds are living descendants of dinosaurs | Birds and dinosaurs were never related | Dinosaurs came from birds | All dinosaurs could fly, just like the birds today
test: why do birds keep picking at their feathers all the time | They preen to keep their feathers clean, tidy and waterproof | They are pulling out feathers to build nests | They are looking for food hidden in them | They are scratching bites from other birds
test: how do birds chew if they got no teeth | A muscly part of the stomach, the gizzard, grinds the food | They chew with hard ridges on their tongue | They only eat soft food like nectar and worms that needs no chewing | Their beaks have tiny teeth too small to see
test: Ava says birds' bones are solid and heavy so they're strong. true? | No, many bird bones are hollow, which makes them light | Yes, heavy bones help birds fly steadily | Yes, but only in birds that cannot fly | No, birds have no bones, only muscle
test: my sister reckons birds have nothing to do with dinosaurs. right? | No, birds are living descendants of dinosaurs | Yes, birds came long before the dinosaurs | Yes, birds are related only to bats | No, birds are a kind of flying reptile
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the material of which feathers are composed? | Keratin, the same material as hair and nails | Bone, the same hard material as the rest of the skeleton | Cartilage, the same material as the ears | Chitin, the same material as insect shells
exam: Identify the principal reason for which many of a bird's bones are hollow. | It renders the bird light for flight | It permits the bird to store water | It renders the bird warmer in winter | It permits the bird to float upon water
exam: Ascertain the manner in which a bird grinds its food in the absence of teeth. | By the gizzard, a muscly part of the stomach, often with grit | By the beak, which chews the food thoroughly | By the tongue, pressed against the palate | By the claws, which tear the food into dust
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cuckoo. | It lays its eggs in other birds' nests to be raised by them | It builds nests of mud beneath the eaves of houses | It carries its eggs in a pouch until they hatch | It buries its eggs in warm sand
